/* ============================ reference ============================ */
const HELP = {"corender": {"t": "Co-rendering two attributes", "c": "concept", "h": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>One attribute is drawn as a background image and a second is painted over it with an opacity that can follow the overlay's own value. Nothing is computed that either attribute did not already contain; the pair is put in one place rather than in two panels, so a spatial relationship between them is read directly instead of by moving the eye and holding one image in memory.</p><h3>The opacity rule</h3><p>A flat opacity paints the overlay everywhere, which hides the background wherever the overlay is uninformative. The other three tie opacity to the overlay itself. High values opaque suits an attribute whose interest is at the top of its range, such as energy or bandwidth. Low values opaque suits coherence and linearity, where the meaning is in the breaks rather than in the continuous parts. Distance from the middle suits a signed attribute, where zero is the uninteresting state and both polarities matter.</p><p>The threshold sets where the ramp begins: below it the overlay is fully transparent, and at the top of the range it reaches the opacity chosen. The caption reports the mean opacity over the crop and the fraction that is more than half overlay. Below about ten percent the overlay is a sparse marking on the background; above about seventy the background is no longer being read, and the display is the overlay with a texture behind it.</p><h3>Two color bars</h3><p>Both scales are drawn beside the panel because a blended pixel carries two quantities and neither bar describes it alone. A color read off either bar is the value that pixel would have had at full opacity for that layer, not the value it has.</p><h3>Limitations</h3><p>Blending is not additive information. Two attributes that correlate produce a display that looks confirmatory while showing one measurement twice, which is what the correlation matrix under a multi-attribute grid is for. A color appearing in the blend that belongs to neither bar is an artifact of the mixing and means nothing. And an overlay whose opacity follows its own value is a thresholded display: features just below the threshold are absent, not weak, so moving the threshold is part of reading it rather than a matter of taste.</p>"}, "stripe": {"t": "Trace-to-trace scatter", "c": "concept", "h": "<h3>What is measured</h3><p>The correlation between each trace of an attribute panel and the trace next to it, at zero lag. A value near 1 means neighboring traces carry the same thing, which is what a laterally continuous geological feature produces. A low value means the panel changes from one trace to the next, which appears as vertical striping.</p><h3>Why some attributes stripe and others do not</h3><p>Average frequency, average bandwidth, instantaneous frequency and the phase attributes are computed one trace at a time. No part of their definition looks sideways, so any lateral continuity they show was already in the input and any scatter in the input passes straight through. RMS amplitude and the amplitude volume transform are also computed trace by trace but average over a window in time, which suppresses the scatter that survives into them. Apparent dip, linearity and coherence do look sideways, so a low value there is a statement about the section rather than about the attribute.</p><h3>What raises it</h3><p>Lateral continuity has to come from the input, since the attribute will not supply it. Two filters do that, and both have a cost that the removed panel reports.</p><p>Rejecting steep dips removes the energy that changes fastest from trace to trace. On COCORP Wyoming Line 1 this raises apparent dip from 0.56 to 0.99 and average frequency from 0.64 to 0.79, and removes 18 percent of the input energy.</p><p>Structure-oriented smoothing averages along the local reflector. Its effect depends almost entirely on the edge-protection gate, which scales the smoothing by linearity raised to a power. On a section whose linearity is low throughout, as a deep crustal record is, the gate holds the filter back nearly everywhere: at the default setting of 2 the same line moves from 0.64 to only 0.68. Lowering the gate to 0.5 gives 0.80 for 26 percent of the input energy, and to 0 gives 0.96 for 36 percent.</p><h3>Limitations</h3><p>The measurement does not distinguish scatter from geology. A section whose character genuinely changes over a few traces reads low, and so does one whose attribute is noise. It also cannot say whether the energy a filter removed to raise it was noise: at an edge-protection gate of 0 the smoothing averages across discontinuities and produces continuous reflectors where the input had breaks, which raises this number while destroying the feature.</p>"}, "bandwidth": {"t": "Bandwidth of the data", "c": "concept", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3><p>Each trace of the crop is tapered over a window, Fourier transformed, and the magnitudes averaged across traces. The average is drawn in decibels relative to the peak of the whole-crop spectrum, so a window whose energy has decayed sits lower on the axis rather than being renormalized to fill it. The same measurement repeated in windows stepped down the section gives the second display, whose vertical axis is record time.</p><h3>The quantities reported</h3><p>The peak frequency is the maximum of the average spectrum. The half-power limits are the frequencies at which the spectrum has fallen 6 decibels below that peak, and the 20 decibel limits bound the range over which the data carries energy above the noise floor. The dominant period is the reciprocal of the peak frequency, and the dominant wavelength is that period multiplied by the velocity entered under Display. A quarter of that wavelength is the conventional limit of separability between two interfaces.</p><h3>In the earth</h3><p>Absorption and scattering remove high frequencies preferentially along the travel path, so on a deep record the peak frequency and the upper half-power limit both fall with time. Legacy 2D profiles start from a narrower band than modern data as well: vibroseis sweeps of the 1970s commonly began near 8 Hz and ended between 30 and 50 Hz, and an archived stack carries whatever the processing sequence of the time left of that. On COCORP Wyoming Line 1, recorded at an 8 ms sample interval, the Nyquist frequency is 62.5 Hz and the data occupy a small part of it.</p><h3>What the numbers govern</h3><p>The pass band, the spectral band attribute and the three-band RGB blend are meaningful only inside the measured band; outside it they operate on noise. The analysis window of the windowed attributes is a length in milliseconds, which is a number of cycles only once the dominant frequency is known. The quarter wavelength sets what thickness the section can separate at all.</p><h3>Limitations</h3><p>The spectrum is averaged over the traces of the crop, so a lateral change in character is averaged into it rather than displayed. The window length is a trade: a short window follows the change with time and resolves frequency coarsely, a long one does the reverse. The measurement includes noise, so at times where the signal has decayed the spectrum shown is the spectrum of the noise.</p>"}, "start": {"t": "Input, kept, and removed", "c": "concept", "h": "<h3>What the panels show</h3> <p>Each filter separates the data into two parts. The <b>kept</b> panel is the filter output. The <b>removed</b> panel is the arithmetic difference between the input and the kept result, so the two panels sum exactly to the input. A figure below the removed panel gives its share of the input energy.</p> <h3>What appears in the removed panel</h3> <p>Whatever the filter rejected. Random noise appears as speckle with no organization. Coherent noise appears as organized events, usually at a dip that differs from the reflectors. Signal appears as reflector geometry: layering, terminations, fault planes.</p> <p>The presence of reflector geometry in the removed panel indicates that the filter has rejected part of the signal at the current parameter setting.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>The removed panel is displayed at its own gain, given on its color bar, so the visual prominence of the residual is not proportional to its energy. The percentage figure is the quantitative measure.</p>"}, "fk": {"t": "f-k dip and frequency rejection", "c": "filter", "h": "<h3>What it does</h3> <p>A two-dimensional Fourier transform maps the section into wavenumber and frequency. An event with a constant slope on the section maps to a line through the origin of that plane, so events with different dips occupy different regions of it. The filter sets a wedge of that plane to zero and transforms back, removing events steeper than a chosen dip.</p> <p>Dip is expressed in samples per trace, which is independent of sample interval and trace spacing. An event descending one sample every ten traces has a dip of 0.1.</p> <h3>Controls</h3> <p><b>Dip limit</b> sets the boundary of the rejected wedge; events steeper than this are removed. <b>Transition width</b> sets the cosine taper across that boundary, which reduces ringing at the cost of a less sharp separation. <b>Pass band</b> applies a frequency filter on the same transform. The spectrum display shades the rejected region.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Reflected energy from subhorizontal interfaces occupies low dips. Steeper dips are occupied by migration artifacts, ground-roll remnants, some multiples, and linear noise trains. On many 2D lines reflectors lie below 0.1 samples per trace, so the separation on dip is clean. Steeply dipping geology, such as crustal thrusts, can reach 0.6 or more and overlaps the range where coherent noise also lies.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>The filter operates on dip alone and cannot distinguish steep signal from steep noise.</p> <p>The operation is not local. A wedge rejection has an impulse response spanning the whole section, so filtering a crop and cropping a filtered line give different results throughout the crop, not only near its edges.</p>"}, "sos": {"t": "Structure-oriented smoothing", "c": "filter", "h": "<h3>What it does</h3> <p>The local orientation of the image is estimated at every sample from the gradient structure tensor, and the data are averaged along that orientation rather than across it. Energy with a consistent local dip is preserved by the averaging; energy without one is reduced.</p> <p>The same tensor yields a linearity between 0 and 1. The edge-protection control raises linearity to a power and uses it to scale the amount of smoothing applied, so smoothing is reduced where the image has no single dominant orientation.</p> <h3>Controls</h3> <p><b>Smoothing length</b> is the distance along the reflector over which the average is taken. <b>Dip estimation window</b> is the size of the region contributing to the orientation estimate. <b>Edge protection</b> is the exponent applied to linearity; at 0 the smoothing is applied uniformly. <b>Strength</b> scales the whole operation.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Reflectors are laterally continuous over distances large compared with the smoothing length, so they survive. Faults, terminations, pinchouts and channel edges are locations where the image has no single orientation, and the linearity gate reduces smoothing there.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>With edge protection at 0, a discontinuity narrower than the smoothing length is averaged across, producing a continuous reflector where the input contained a break.</p> <p>In a region containing only noise the structure tensor still returns an orientation, and averaging along it produces laterally continuous features that were not present in the input.</p>"}, "detail": {"t": "Detail boost", "c": "filter", "h": "<h3>What it does</h3> <p>A Gaussian-smoothed version of the section is subtracted to give a base and a detail component. The detail is multiplied by a gain and added back to the base.</p> <h3>Controls</h3> <p><b>Base scale</b> is the width of the smoothing that defines the split. Features larger than this go to the base, smaller ones to the detail. <b>Detail gain</b> above 1 amplifies the detail component; below 1 attenuates it.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Thin beds, closely spaced reflectors and short terminations are small-scale and fall in the detail component. Regional amplitude variation and broad brightening are large-scale and fall in the base.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>An amplitude anomaly wider than the base scale is a large-scale feature and is placed in the base, so raising the detail gain does not affect it.</p> <p>The gain is applied to whatever occupies the detail component, including noise and any artifacts introduced by a preceding filter.</p>"}, "envelope": {"t": "Envelope", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The magnitude of the analytic trace, formed from the trace and its Hilbert transform. It is the instantaneous amplitude of the seismic signal, independent of phase, so a peak and the adjacent trough of one reflection produce a single envelope maximum rather than two events separated by a null.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>High:</b> large instantaneous amplitude. <b>Low:</b> small instantaneous amplitude. The envelope is bounded below by the absolute value of the trace and is never negative.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Amplitude responds to the magnitude of the impedance contrast at an interface, so high envelope commonly corresponds to a large lithology or fluid change. It also rises where reflections from closely spaced interfaces interfere constructively, which occurs at bed thicknesses near a quarter of the dominant wavelength. Gas-charged intervals frequently produce high envelope through the associated impedance reduction.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>The envelope carries the gain applied to the data, so values at different times or in different surveys are not comparable unless the gain history is known. After AGC the relative amplitude relationships that make the attribute interpretable have been removed.</p> <p>Tuning and a genuine amplitude anomaly both raise the envelope, and the attribute alone does not separate them.</p>"}, "insphase": {"t": "Instantaneous phase", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The argument of the analytic trace, in radians. It records the position within the wavelet cycle at each sample and contains no amplitude information: a weak reflection and a strong one at the same point in their cycles have the same phase.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p>The quantity is cyclic, so high and low have no interpretive meaning. Zero corresponds to a peak, ±π to a trough, and ±π/2 to a zero crossing. The information is in the lateral continuity of the phase bands rather than in their values.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Because amplitude is normalized out, reflections of any strength are displayed equally, so phase bands follow bedding continuously across intervals where amplitude varies. Lateral breaks in a phase band occur at faults, unconformities and stratigraphic terminations.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>The value jumps from +π to −π once per cycle. These discontinuities appear as lateral bands in the display and originate in the representation, not in the data. Cosine of phase and unwrapped phase are formulations without them.</p> <p>Where the envelope approaches zero the phase is determined by whatever is present, including noise, and varies rapidly.</p>"}, "cosphase": {"t": "Cosine of instantaneous phase", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The cosine of the instantaneous phase. It carries the same information as the phase but as a continuous quantity between −1 and +1, with no wrap discontinuity. It is the seismic trace with its amplitude normalized to unity everywhere.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>+1:</b> a peak. <b>−1:</b> a trough. <b>0:</b> a zero crossing. All reflections are displayed at the same strength regardless of their amplitude.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>The display follows bedding continuity into intervals where reflections are too weak to see on an amplitude section, which makes it used for tracing horizons through low-amplitude zones and for identifying terminations and onlap geometry.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>Amplitude normalization applies equally to noise. In an interval with low signal-to-noise ratio, the attribute displays noise at the same strength as signal and produces banding with no reflection origin. The envelope indicates where the signal is strong enough for the display to be meaningful.</p>"}, "insfreq": {"t": "Instantaneous frequency", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The time derivative of the instantaneous phase, divided by 2π, in Hz. It estimates the dominant frequency of the signal at each individual sample rather than averaged over a window.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>High:</b> the local waveform is oscillating rapidly. <b>Low:</b> it is oscillating slowly. The estimate can take negative values, which have no physical interpretation as a frequency.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Frequency content relates to bed thickness, since thinner beds produce interference patterns with higher dominant frequency, and to attenuation, which removes high frequencies preferentially with depth and with path length through absorptive material. Localised low-frequency zones beneath high-amplitude reflections have been associated with gas, though scattering, thin-bed interference and tuning also produce them.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>The estimate is a ratio whose denominator is the squared envelope. Where the envelope approaches zero the ratio is unstable, and the attribute takes very large positive and negative values. On a typical section around a third of samples fall outside the range 0 to Nyquist, with excursions of several orders of magnitude. This behavior is a property of the definition rather than an artifact of a particular implementation.</p> <p>Wavelet frequency and average frequency are stabilised measurements of the same quantity.</p>"}, "unwrap": {"t": "Unwrapped phase", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The instantaneous phase accumulated continuously along the trace rather than folded into the interval ±π. The phase derivative is integrated directly, so the result never wraps. Its slope at any point equals 2π times the instantaneous frequency.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p>The absolute value depends on an arbitrary integration constant and is not interpretable. The interpretable quantity is the local gradient: a steeper gradient corresponds to a higher instantaneous frequency.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Departures of the gradient from its regional trend indicate intervals where the frequency content differs from its surroundings, arising from the same causes as for instantaneous frequency: bed thickness, attenuation and interference.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>The attribute is an integral, so an error in the phase derivative at one time propagates to every later sample. Absolute levels deep in a record accumulate the errors above them.</p>"}, "sweetness": {"t": "Sweetness", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The envelope divided by the square root of the instantaneous frequency. It is a composite of two other attributes and increases with amplitude and decreases with frequency.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>High:</b> high amplitude, low frequency, or both. <b>Low:</b> low amplitude, high frequency, or both.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Radovich and Oliveros (1998) observed in the Gulf of Mexico that sandy facies embedded in a shale matrix frequently show both higher envelope and lower instantaneous frequency than the surrounding shale, and combined the two into this single quantity for that reason. The association is empirical and was established in a particular geological setting.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>Because the two contributions multiply, a high value can arise from either alone. A tuned reflector of ordinary frequency and a weak low-frequency interval can produce the same value. The envelope and frequency examined separately distinguish these cases.</p> <p>The attribute inherits the gain dependence of the envelope.</p>"}, "wavfreq": {"t": "Wavelet frequency", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>Also called response frequency. The envelope maxima are located, and the instantaneous frequency at each maximum is assigned to every sample between the adjacent envelope minima. The result is one frequency value per reflection event, constant across that event.</p> <p>The reasoning, from Bodine (1984), is that most of the energy in a trace lies near the envelope peaks, which is where the frequency estimate is best conditioned.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>High:</b> a rapidly oscillating reflection event. <b>Low:</b> a slowly oscillating one. The range is bounded in practice because the estimate is taken only where the envelope is large.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>The same relationships as for instantaneous frequency: bed thickness and attenuation. The measurement is expressed per event rather than per sample.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>The display is piecewise constant by construction and cannot resolve variation within a single reflection event.</p>"}, "wavphase": {"t": "Wavelet phase", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The instantaneous phase at each envelope maximum, assigned to every sample between the adjacent envelope minima. It describes the phase character of each reflection event with one value.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>Near 0:</b> the event is centered on a peak, consistent with a zero-phase wavelet and a positive reflection coefficient. <b>Near ±π:</b> centered on a trough, consistent with a negative reflection coefficient. <b>Near ±π/2:</b> the event is not centered on either, which occurs for non-zero-phase wavelets and for thin-bed responses.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>A lateral change from near 0 to near ±π along one reflector is consistent with a polarity reversal, which occurs where the sign of the impedance contrast changes, as at a fluid contact or a lateral facies change.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>Wavelet phase also varies with the phase of the source wavelet left by processing, which can vary spatially. A phase change measured on one reflector does not by itself establish a change in reflection coefficient.</p>"}, "avgfreq": {"t": "Average frequency", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The instantaneous frequency averaged over a window and weighted by instantaneous power, so samples with strong signal contribute in proportion to their energy and samples with none contribute little. Following Barnes (2016).</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>High:</b> the energy in the window is concentrated at higher frequencies. <b>Low:</b> at lower frequencies. The weighting keeps the estimate within the data bandwidth in normal circumstances.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Average frequency declines with depth in most sections as the earth attenuates high frequencies along the travel path. Departures from that regional trend indicate local change: thinner bedding raises it, and absorptive or scattering intervals lower it.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>The window length determines what is measured. A short window follows individual reflection events; a long one characterises intervals. Values from different window lengths are not comparable.</p>"}, "avgband": {"t": "Average bandwidth", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The spread of the power-weighted instantaneous frequency distribution within the window, expressed as twice its standard deviation. Where average frequency gives the center of that distribution, bandwidth gives its width.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>High:</b> the instantaneous frequency varies substantially within the window. <b>Low:</b> it is close to constant.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Variation in instantaneous frequency within a window arises from interference between reflections at different spacings, so high bandwidth commonly corresponds to thinly interbedded or heterogeneous intervals. Low bandwidth corresponds to isolated reflections and to monotonous sections.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>The convention used here is the full bandwidth, twice the standard deviation. Barnes (2016) defines the bandwidth as the standard deviation itself, so values differ by a factor of two between the two conventions.</p> <p>The measurement describes the variation of the instantaneous frequency, which is not the same quantity as the width of the amplitude spectrum. A signal composed of two symmetric tones has a constant instantaneous frequency and therefore near-zero bandwidth by this measure, while its amplitude spectrum is wide.</p>"}, "rms": {"t": "RMS amplitude", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The root mean square of the trace within a running window. It is the square root of the mean power in the window.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>High:</b> large mean power in the window. <b>Low:</b> small mean power. The quantity is never negative.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Intervals containing many strong reflections read high, and intervals with few or weak reflections read low. On deep reflection data this is the measurement underlying descriptions of crust as reflective or transparent.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>The window length changes the result. A window comparable to the wavelet length approaches the envelope and follows individual reflections; a longer window characterises intervals.</p> <p>The measurement includes noise, so an interval can read high because its signal-to-noise ratio is low rather than because it contains reflections. Measures based on spatial coherence separate the two.</p> <p>Like all amplitude measurements, it carries the applied gain.</p>"}, "tke": {"t": "Teager-Kaiser energy", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The square of the first time derivative of the trace, minus the product of the trace and its second derivative. For a signal of amplitude A and angular frequency ω the operator returns A²ω² exactly.</p> <p>Kaiser (1990) derived it from the energy of a mass on a spring. The seismic energy density given by Sheriff and Geldart (1995) has the same dependence on amplitude and frequency, which is the basis for using the operator as an estimator of it. The attribute therefore responds to amplitude and frequency jointly, unlike RMS amplitude, which responds to amplitude alone.</p> <p>Applied to the analytic trace, the real and imaginary parts each contribute A²ω², so the complex-trace result is twice the real-trace value (Hamila et al., 1999).</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>High:</b> large amplitude, high frequency, or both. <b>Low:</b> small amplitude, low frequency, or both. The output is non-negative. Two reflections of equal amplitude but different frequency give different values.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>The attribute is high where strong reflections coincide with well-resolved, higher-frequency character, and low in weak or attenuated intervals. Because it is a squared quantity its own frequency content is lower than that of the input, so it mixes vertically over a longer interval than the reflection data.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>The output is non-negative and therefore has a non-zero mean, which prevents its use as input to coherence and similar attributes. The Teager-Kaiser variation is the zero-mean formulation.</p> <p>The derivatives are computed with smooth noise-robust filters, which are low-pass by construction and under-estimate the derivative as frequency rises. On 2 ms data the 7-point filter returns 0.988 of the true derivative at 10 Hz and 0.806 at 45 Hz, so the energy, being quadratic in the derivative, reads about 35 percent low at 45 Hz. Values are therefore not directly comparable across widely separated frequencies. A shorter filter reduces the effect and is less resistant to noise.</p>"}, "tkv": {"t": "Teager-Kaiser variation", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The Teager-Kaiser energy, bandpass filtered and then Hilbert transformed. The filtering removes the non-zero mean and the Hilbert transform converts the result into an oscillatory quantity. Following Matos (2018).</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p>The quantity oscillates about zero. Zero crossings occur where the underlying energy changes most rapidly with time, so the transitions rather than the extrema mark the boundaries between intervals of differing energy.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Boundaries between packages of differing reflection energy: the tops and bases of reflective intervals, unconformities, and lateral changes in reflection character.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>Its frequency content is lower than that of the input, so it resolves less detail.</p> <p>Polarity information is lost at the energy step, so the sign of the output derives from the transform rather than from the reflection coefficient.</p>"}, "avt": {"t": "Amplitude volume transform", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>Three operations, from Bulhões (1999). The envelope is computed, its RMS is taken in a short window, and an inverse Hilbert transform is applied to that RMS envelope. The last step converts a smooth non-negative quantity into an oscillatory zero-mean one that responds to vertical change in the energy.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p>The quantity oscillates about zero. Zero crossings mark where the RMS envelope changes most rapidly with time, so the display outlines the boundaries of energy packages rather than their interiors.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>The attribute was developed for mapping structural and stratigraphic features and is applied to faults, channels, carbonate bodies, unconformities and reflector terminations, all of which involve abrupt vertical or lateral change in reflection energy.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>The output has lower frequency content than the input. The window length controls this: shorter windows retain more detail and more noise.</p> <p>Polarity is discarded at the envelope step, so the sign of the output is a property of the transform rather than of the reflection coefficient.</p>"}, "rai": {"t": "Relative acoustic impedance", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The trace integrated with respect to time in the frequency domain, then bandpass filtered. Seismic amplitude approximates the reflection coefficient, which is a function of the change in acoustic impedance across an interface; integration recovers a quantity proportional to the impedance of the layer itself, within the seismic bandwidth.</p> <p>Integration divides the spectrum by iω, which rotates the phase by −90 degrees, amplifies low frequencies and attenuates high ones. The bandpass filter limits the amplification of frequencies below those present in the data.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>High:</b> higher acoustic impedance relative to the surrounding section. <b>Low:</b> lower. The phase rotation places layer boundaries at zero crossings and layer interiors at extrema, so the display represents layers rather than contacts.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Acoustic impedance is the product of velocity and density, so high values commonly correspond to carbonates, tight or cemented sands and other consolidated lithologies, and low values to shales, porous sands and gas-charged intervals.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>The integration constant is not recoverable and the low frequencies are absent from seismic data, so the result is relative within the passband and has no absolute datum. Two intervals of genuinely different impedance can display the same level.</p> <p>The derivation assumes a zero-phase wavelet approximating a spike and small reflection coefficients. Departures from these assumptions, particularly residual wavelet phase, are carried into the result.</p>"}, "dip": {"t": "Apparent dip", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The local slope of the reflectors in samples per trace, taken from the eigenvector of the gradient structure tensor associated with the smaller eigenvalue. This is the same estimate that steers the structure-oriented smoothing.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p>The sign gives the direction of dip within the plane of the section and the magnitude its steepness. Zero is a horizontal event.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Smoothly varying dip maps folds and regional structural gradient. Abrupt lateral changes in dip occur at faults, and angular relationships between dip domains occur at unconformities.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>On a 2D line the quantity is apparent dip in the direction of the line. A reflector dipping partly out of the plane of the section reads shallower than its true dip, and the true dip cannot be recovered without a second azimuth.</p> <p>Where no coherent event is present the tensor still returns an orientation. The linearity attribute indicates where the dip estimate is supported by the data.</p>"}, "linearity": {"t": "Linearity", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The difference of the two structure-tensor eigenvalues divided by their sum, giving a value between 0 and 1. It quantifies how strongly the local image is dominated by a single orientation.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>Near 1:</b> the image has one clear orientation, as for a well-defined reflector. <b>Near 0:</b> no preferred orientation, as for random noise, chaotic reflection character, or the intersection of events with different dips.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>High linearity corresponds to continuous layered reflections. Low linearity corresponds to disrupted zones, chaotic facies such as mass transport deposits, and intervals where the signal-to-noise ratio is low.</p> <h3>Not the same as nonparallelism</h3> <p>Both come from the gradient structure tensor and they answer different questions. Linearity is computed from the eigenvalues of the tensor at one location and measures how strongly a single orientation dominates there: it is a statement about whether a dip can be estimated at all. Nonparallelism is computed from how much the estimated dip vectors disagree with one another across a neighborhood, and measures whether reflectors that are individually well defined are running parallel to each other.</p> <p>The two come apart in both directions. Across an angular unconformity, or in a fold whose limbs steepen, every dip estimate can be well determined and linearity high while nonparallelism is large. In a noisy or chaotic interval linearity is low, and nonparallelism computed there compares dip estimates that nothing supports, so its value is not meaningful whatever it reads.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>Low linearity does not distinguish geological disruption from noise, since neither has a dominant orientation.</p> <p>Only linearity is computed here. Nonparallelism is one of the AASPI geometric attribute programs and is not implemented in this tool.</p>"}, "coherence": {"t": "In-line coherence", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>Semblance computed along the local dip across a small group of adjacent traces. It is the ratio of the energy of the summed traces to the summed energy of the individual traces, and quantifies waveform similarity between neighboring traces once their dip has been accounted for.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>Near 1:</b> adjacent traces are similar, corresponding to a laterally continuous reflection. <b>Low:</b> adjacent traces differ. Unlike most attributes, the low values carry the structural information.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>Lateral waveform change occurs at faults, channel margins, reef edges, and stratigraphic terminations, all of which produce low coherence.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>On a 2D line the measurement is made only along the line. A fault striking parallel to the section produces no waveform difference between adjacent traces on the same side of it and is not detected. This is the principal reason coherence is normally computed on 3D volumes.</p> <p>Low signal-to-noise ratio reduces trace similarity and lowers coherence independently of structure.</p>"}, "band": {"t": "Spectral band", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3> <p>The envelope of a single narrow frequency band, isolated with a constant-Q Gaussian filter so that the time resolution scales with the center frequency. Stepping the center frequency through the data bandwidth shows how energy is distributed across frequency.</p> <h3>High and low values</h3> <p><b>High:</b> substantial energy at that frequency at that location. <b>Low:</b> little. Values from different bands are comparable only within the range where the data carries energy.</p> <h3>In the earth</h3> <p>A bed reaches maximum reflection amplitude at the frequency whose quarter wavelength matches its thickness, so thin beds are most prominent in higher bands and thicker beds in lower ones. Comparison across bands is therefore sensitive to layer thickness. Intervals bright at low frequency and weak at high frequency indicate preferential loss of high frequencies, which arises from absorption, from scattering in heterogeneous media, and from thin-bed interference.</p> <h3>Limitations</h3> <p>Bands outside the range where the data has energy contain mostly noise. The spectrum shown in the f-k display gives the range within which band comparison is meaningful.</p>"}, "rgb": {"t": "Three-band RGB blend", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3><p>Three narrow frequency bands are extracted with constant-Q Gaussian filters, their envelopes taken, and each mapped to one channel of a color image: the low band to red, the middle to green, the high to blue. Each channel is scaled to its own 99th percentile, so the three are displayed at comparable strength.</p><h3>High and low values</h3><p>Color encodes which part of the spectrum carries the energy rather than how much there is. <b>Warm colors</b> indicate the low band dominates locally, <b>blue</b> that the high band does, and <b>gray or white</b> that all three are comparable. Dark areas have little energy in any band.</p><h3>In the earth</h3><p>Because a bed reaches maximum amplitude at the frequency whose quarter wavelength matches its thickness, a lateral change in color along one interval corresponds to a change in the thickness or spacing of the layering producing it. A downward shift from blue toward red follows the preferential loss of high frequencies with depth, from absorption and from scattering. On legacy deep data the usable bandwidth is narrow, so the three bands are placed around the chosen center frequency rather than spread across the whole spectrum.</p><h3>Limitations</h3><p>The independent scaling of the three channels means color is relative within each band and not comparable between surveys or between parameter settings. Bands outside the range where the data carries energy contribute noise to their channel, which appears as a uniform color cast.</p><p>The display has no single numeric value, so no color bar is drawn and the hover readout does not apply.</p>"}, "display": {"t": "Amplitude, scale and vertical exaggeration", "c": "concept", "h": "<h3>Amplitude</h3><p>Three panels share one gain field, computed from the input and applied to all of them, so the removed panel is displayed at the same scale as what it came from.</p><p><b>As recorded</b> applies nothing. <b>Time gain</b> multiplies by t raised to a power, one smooth curve applied to every trace alike, which counters spherical divergence and attenuation while leaving lateral amplitude relations intact. <b>AGC</b> normalizes by the local RMS in a window, which opens up the deep record further but scales every sample by a different factor.</p><p>The distinction matters for the amplitude-based attributes. Under a t-power gain the envelope, sweetness and relative acoustic impedance remain comparable from trace to trace, because the gain depends only on time. Under AGC they do not.</p><h3>Vertical exaggeration</h3><p>The ratio between the vertical and horizontal scales of the display. At 1x the section is drawn true to scale and a dip measured off it is the dip in the ground. Reflection sections are conventionally shown near 3x, which makes bedding easier to follow at the cost of steepening every dip.</p><p>The exaggeration is computed from the trace spacing and a velocity. Legacy trace headers rarely record the spacing, so it is entered by hand; COCORP CDP spacing is typically 50 to 100 m. The depth column on the time axis uses one velocity for the whole section and is a scale rather than a conversion.</p><h3>Limitations</h3><p>A dip read from an exaggerated display is not the dip of the reflector. At 3x, a bed dipping 33 degrees is drawn at 63. On an unmigrated section the reflector is also not in its true position, so both corrections apply.</p>"}, "compare": {"t": "Comparing several attributes", "c": "concept", "h": "<h3>What the panel shows</h3><p>Any selection of attributes computed on the current crop and drawn on a shared time axis. The intermediates several of them need, the analytic trace and the structure tensor, are computed once for the whole set, so eight attributes on a 500,000-sample crop take well under a second.</p><h3>The correlation matrix</h3><p>Pearson correlation between every displayed pair, on a subsample. It answers a question that the panels themselves cannot: whether the attributes are measuring different things.</p><p>Measured on COCORP Wyoming Line 1, envelope correlates 0.98 with RMS amplitude, 0.97 with sweetness and 0.91 with Teager-Kaiser energy. Those four are one measurement expressed four ways. Displaying them side by side gives four panels but one dimension, and anything computed from the set as though it had four will be dominated by amplitude. Coherence and linearity correlate 0.78 on the same data, both being derived from the structure tensor.</p><p>The default selection is chosen so that no pair exceeds 0.5.</p><h3>Limitations</h3><p>Correlation is computed on the signed values. A signed attribute and a magnitude can read near zero while their magnitudes track each other closely, so a low number is not on its own proof of independence.</p><p>Pearson correlation measures linear association only. Two attributes related through a curve can read low and still carry the same information.</p><p>The attributes are computed on the kept section, so the correlations reflect the filters currently applied as well as the data.</p>"}, "som": {"t": "Self-organizing map", "c": "concept", "h": "<h3>What it does</h3><p>A grid of nodes is fitted to the displayed attributes so that each node comes to represent a recurring combination of attribute values, and nodes near each other on the grid represent similar combinations. Every sample is then labeled with its closest node. The attributes are standardised to zero mean and unit variance first, without which the distance is dominated by whichever attribute carries the largest numbers.</p><p>Node color follows position on the grid, so neighboring classes take neighboring colors and the image reads as a continuum rather than as unrelated categories. The legend beside the section shows the grid, each node drawn at a size proportional to how many samples it took.</p><h3>Reading one class at a time</h3><p>The key beside the section is the control as well as the legend. Clicking a node hides that class, clicking it again brings it back, and alt-clicking shows that node alone; alt-clicking it a second time restores the rest. A hidden class is replaced by the section itself in pale gray rather than by a blank, so the classes still on screen keep the structure they sit in. The caption reports how much of the crop the visible classes cover.</p><p>A map of thirty-six or sixty-four classes is more than can be read at once, and most questions asked of one concern two or three of them: whether a class follows a horizon, whether it stops at a fault, whether two classes that took neighboring colors are in fact in the same place. Those are questions about where a class goes, which the full map answers badly because every other class is drawn over the same section.</p><h3>The null test</h3><p>A clustering method always returns clusters. Run on data with no spatial structure it produces classes that look as organized as those from data with plenty, so the picture alone cannot say which case is in front of you.</p><p>The test compares how often adjacent traces share a class against the same figure for a null: the same attributes with the phase of each trace randomised independently. That leaves every attribute's amplitude spectrum and histogram intact while destroying the relationship between one trace and the next. A ratio near 1 means the classes are no more laterally organized than they would be with the lateral information removed.</p><p>Measured on a 330,000-sample crop of Wyoming Line 1 with six attributes and an 8x8 map, adjacent traces share a class 27.7 percent of the time against 2.0 percent for the null, a ratio of 14. On data constructed to have no structure at all the same procedure returns 1.08.</p><h3>Limitations</h3><p>The comparison is lateral only, because the null preserves each trace's own spectrum and so preserves vertical smoothness. Counting vertical agreement would measure smoothness rather than organization.</p><p>Correlated attributes count once, not once each. Four amplitude-derived attributes give a map trained on one dimension expressed four ways; the correlation matrix above shows which.</p><p>The classes have no meaning attached to them. The map says which samples resemble each other across the chosen attributes, not what any group is made of.</p><p>Training uses a subsample of up to 50,000 points and a fixed seed, so a run repeats exactly; changing the crop, the attributes or the map size changes the classes and their colors entirely.</p>"}, "relief": {"t": "Relief shading", "c": "attribute", "h": "<h3>What it measures</h3><p>Nothing the amplitude did not already contain. The section is treated as a surface whose height at each point is the amplitude, and a directional light is shone across it; the image is the Lambertian response of that surface. This is analytical hillshading, the standard method for rendering terrain relief, applied to seismic. Lynch applies it to seismic under the name High Visual Resolution Interpretation.</p><p>Two things are taken out of the height before the light is shone on it. The oscillation, by building the surface from the envelope rather than the amplitude, so it rises once per reflection instead of once per half cycle. And the level, by dividing by a long vertical average of the envelope, so a record whose amplitude falls by orders of magnitude does not come out flat below the first second. What is left is the shape of the surface, which is what the shading is for.</p><h3>High and low values</h3><p>Bright is a face turned toward the light, dark is a face turned away. A flat area returns the sine of the light elevation exactly. The values are illumination, not amplitude: a bright area is a slope facing the lamp, not a strong reflection.</p><h3>What it responds to</h3><p>Lateral change in the <i>shape</i> of the amplitude surface. Ridge height follows amplitude, ridge width and spacing follow bed thickness and tuning, ridge tilt follows dip, and a ridge ends at a termination. Thickness and tuning changes are therefore expressed strongly, and they are among the things a color bar flattens most.</p><p>On a synthetic containing a tuning wedge, a gradual brightening and a fault, the lateral swing through each feature measured in units of the display's own spread was 6.8 times larger on the shaded image than on the amplitude for the wedge, 2.4 times for the brightening and 15.5 times for the fault. Lateral detail through the wedge was 20 times stronger.</p><h3>Controls</h3><p><b>Azimuth</b> is the direction the light comes from, 0 from the top and 90 from the right. Amplitude ridges run along bedding, so lighting across bedding renders the layering while lighting along it leaves the layering flat and renders lateral change instead. <b>Elevation</b> controls contrast, low angles giving long shadows. <b>Surface</b> chooses the height field. The envelope does not change sign and is the one that works on data with few samples per cycle; the amplitude is the literal section and is kept for comparison. <b>Smoothing</b> is measured in wavelet periods rather than samples, so the same setting means the same thing on an 8 ms legacy line and a 2 ms modern one, and it is wider down the trace than across it: the corrugation to be removed is a length in samples, the lateral detail to be kept is a length in traces. <b>Relief</b> is the vertical exaggeration. Until the slider is moved it is solved for rather than set, by finding the exaggeration that puts two percent of the image on the ambient floor. A vertical scale means nothing on its own, since it is a height divided by the percentile of a surface whose units change with the smoothing, the sample interval and the choice of surface; the fraction in full shadow reads the same on every dataset. On Wyoming Line 1 the solution is about 1.75 on the envelope and 1.45 on the amplitude.</p><h3>Limitations</h3><p>The method needs a well-behaved surface more than it needs any particular kind of feature. It works where reflectors are isolated against a quiet background and the wavelet is well sampled in time. On COCORP Wyoming Line 1 a wavelet cycle spans 8.8 samples at 14 Hz and an 8 ms sample interval, and the amplitude surface still renders a herringbone of the wavelet across the whole section. The envelope surface does not, because the thing being sampled 8.8 times is no longer in it. Below about six samples per cycle nothing rescues the shading, since by then the envelope itself is barely resolved.</p><p>Nothing seen here is evidence on its own, because the shading adds no information. A feature noticed in this display should be confirmed on the amplitude or on an attribute before it is interpreted.</p><p>The height field is built from the filtered section, not from the display, so the result no longer depends on the gain: dividing by the long average of the envelope does the same job a display gain does, and does it before the light is applied. A display gain still changes the amplitude panel it is being compared against.</p>"}, "color": {"t": "Adjusting the color scale", "c": "concept", "h": "<h3>Opening the editor</h3><p>Click the scale strip under any attribute panel, or the color bar beside the single attribute panel. The editor appears above the grid and applies to that attribute alone. Nothing is recomputed: only the mapping from value to color changes, so it responds immediately.</p><h3>The controls</h3><p><b>Clip</b> sets the range from a percentile of the attribute's own values, and is the quickest fix for a display dominated by a few extreme samples. At the 99th percentile one percent of samples sit at an end of the bar; at the 95th, five percent do. For a signed attribute the range stays symmetric about zero, so zero keeps its color and polarity stays readable.</p><p><b>Min</b> and <b>Max</b> set the range directly, which is what to use when comparing two crops or two lines: the automatic range is computed per attribute per crop, so the same color means different values in different panels until you fix it by hand.</p><p><b>Invert</b> reverses the color map. <b>Reset</b> returns to the automatic range.</p><h3>Limitations</h3><p>Clipping does not remove the extreme values, it only stops them setting the scale. A bright feature that saturates is still bright; it is no longer distinguishable from anything else that saturates.</p><p>Choices are held per attribute and are cleared when the crop, the file or the attribute set changes, since the ranges would no longer correspond to the same data.</p><p>The three seismic panels use the section color and clip controls in the Display tab, not this editor, so that input, kept and removed stay on one scale.</p>"}};

const HELP_ORDER = ["start", "display", "compare", "som", "color", "fk", "sos", "detail", "envelope", "insphase", "cosphase", "insfreq", "unwrap", "sweetness", "wavfreq", "wavphase", "avgfreq", "avgband", "rms", "tke", "tkv", "avt", "rai", "dip", "linearity", "coherence", "band", "rgb", "relief"];

/* Reference text opens in its own window, so it can sit beside the tool while
   the controls are being worked. One window is reused for every topic. */
function helpDocument(){
  const groups = [["concept","How to read this tool"],
                  ["filter","Filters"],
                  ["attribute","Attributes"]];
  let nav = "", body = "";
  for (const [cat, label] of groups){
    const keys = HELP_ORDER.filter(k => HELP[k].c === cat);
    if (!keys.length) continue;
    nav += "<h4>" + label + "</h4><ul>";
    for (const k of keys){
      nav += '<li><a href="#' + k + '">' + HELP[k].t + "</a></li>";
      body += '<section id="' + k + '"><h2>' + HELP[k].t + "</h2>" + HELP[k].h +
              '<p class="back"><a href="#top">Back to the top</a></p></section>';
    }
    nav += "</ul>";
  }
  return `<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
<link rel="stylesheet" media="print" onload="this.media='all'"
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Archivo:wght@600;700&family=IBM+Plex+Sans:wght@400;600&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400&display=swap">
<title>Deep Reflection \u2014 reference</title>
<style>
:root{--ink:#16191C;--slate:#5C6670;--rule:#C9CDD2;--fill:#F4F6F7;--red:#841617}
*{box-sizing:border-box}
body{margin:0;font:15px/1.6 system-ui,-apple-system,"Segoe UI",Roboto,sans-serif;
  color:var(--ink);background:#fff}
header{padding:1rem 1.4rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);background:var(--fill)}
header h1{margin:0;font-size:1rem;font-weight:600}
header p{margin:.2rem 0 0;font-size:.82rem;color:var(--slate)}
main{padding:1.2rem 1.4rem 4rem;max-width:64ch}
nav{padding:1rem 1.4rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);font-size:.86rem}
nav h4{margin:.7rem 0 .25rem;font-size:.74rem;color:var(--slate);font-weight:600}
nav h4:first-child{margin-top:0}
nav ul{margin:0;padding-left:1.1rem}
nav li{margin:.12rem 0}
nav a{color:var(--ink);text-decoration:none}
nav a:hover{color:var(--red);text-decoration:underline}
section{padding-top:1.2rem;margin-bottom:2rem;border-top:1px solid var(--rule)}
section:first-of-type{border-top:0;padding-top:0}
h2{font-size:1rem;color:var(--red);margin:0 0 .5rem;
  letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase}
h3{font-size:.86rem;margin:1.1rem 0 .3rem}
p{margin:.55rem 0}
b{font-weight:600}
.back{font-size:.78rem}
.back a{color:var(--slate)}
:target{animation:flash 1.4s ease-out}
@keyframes flash{from{background:#fdf2f2}to{background:transparent}}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){:target{animation:none}}
</style></head><body id="top">
<header><h1>Deep Reflection \u2014 reference</h1>
<p>What each control and attribute does, and how to read the result.</p></header>
<nav>${nav}</nav><main>${body}</main></body></html>`;
}
